course: world
lesson: 110
title: Why time flies and why it drags
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Clocks tick at the same speed whatever you are doing, but your feeling of time does not. Maya plays Minecraft "for 10 minutes", yet the clock says 4:00 to 5:10, which is 70 minutes. When you are having fun and concentrating hard, you stop noticing time, so it seems to fly.
explain: When you are bored or worried you keep noticing every second, so time seems to drag. In maths Josh stares at the clock: "It's moved one minute in, like, an hour. This clock's knackered!" ("knackered" means broken or worn out). The clock is fine; his brain is counting every tick.
explain: Twenty minutes of Fortnite feels "quick" and twenty minutes of homework feels "forever", but both are exactly 20 minutes. Scary moments can feel long too: one idea is that in a fright the brain stores many details, so afterwards the moment seems to have lasted longer. Scientists study this by asking people to guess how long things took.
explain: Looking back works the other way round from waiting. Grandad says the years fly by when you get older. Nobody knows the full reason, but one idea is that at 10 a year is one tenth of your whole life, while at 70 it is only one seventieth; another is that children have lots of new things to remember, and new memories make a year feel bigger.
explain: Games have their own clocks: a Minecraft day and night lasts 20 real minutes, so 2 real hours hold 6 Minecraft days. Game time and real time are different clocks.
fact: A clock runs at the same speed all the time; it is our feeling of time that speeds up or slows down.
fact: When people enjoy something and concentrate hard, time seems to pass quickly: "time flies when you're having fun".
fact: When people are bored or keep checking the clock, time seems to drag.
fact: From 4:00 to 5:10 is 1 hour 10 minutes, which is 70 minutes.
fact: In Minecraft a full day and night lasts 20 real minutes, so 2 hours (120 minutes) hold 6 Minecraft days and 1 hour holds 3.
fact: A strategy game that passes 50 years in 60 real minutes passes a bit less than one year every real minute.
fact: Worry and fear can make a short time feel long; afterwards a frightening moment often seems to have lasted longer than it did.
fact: Scientists study the feeling of time with experiments that ask people to guess how long something took.
fact: At age 10 one year is a tenth of your life so far, while at 70 it is a seventieth; this is one idea for why years feel faster as people age, but scientists are not sure.
fact: New experiences make lots of new memories, and looking back, a time full of new memories feels longer.
fact: A 3-hour car ride is 180 minutes, so asking "are we there yet?" every 10 minutes means asking 18 times.
fact: From one Christmas to the next is 365 days, or 366 in a leap year, even if it feels quicker every year.
fact: Words people use: "knackered" means broken or very tired (UK), "in the zone" means so focused you forget everything else, "dreich" means dull and grey (Scotland), "it's giving" means "it seems like" (young US slang) "boss" means great and "sound" means fine or OK (Liverpool, Manchester).
exercise: Maya started playing at 4:00 and it is 5:10. How long has she played? | 70 minutes | 10 minutes | 50 minutes | 110 minutes
exercise: Why did Maya think she had played for only 10 minutes? | Fun and concentration made her stop noticing time | The clock was running too fast | Minecraft stops real clocks while you play | She can't read the clock
exercise: Josh says the maths clock moved one minute in "an hour". What is really happening? | The clock is fine; boredom makes time drag | The clock really is broken | Clocks slow down a little bit in maths lessons | A minute really is longer in school
exercise: A Minecraft day lasts 20 real minutes. How many Minecraft days are there in 2 real hours? | 6 | 2 | 12 | 40
exercise: How many Minecraft days pass in 1 real hour? | 3 | 20 | 1 | 6
exercise: Oscar's brother plays 50 game years in 1 real hour. About how much game time passes each real minute? | A bit less than one year | About 50 years every minute | About one month of game time | Exactly one game day each time
exercise: Lexi's 10-minute wait at the dentist feels like an hour. What makes it feel so long? | Worry and boredom slow down the feeling of time | The dentist's clock runs slow | Dentists make every minute longer on purpose | Waiting rooms are in a different time zone
exercise: Lexi plays with her cousin for an hour and it feels like 10 minutes. Why? | She was having fun, so time seemed to fly | The hour was really only about 10 minutes long | Her cousin secretly moved the clock forward | Playing stops the clock from ticking for a while
exercise: At age 10, what part of your life so far is one year? | A tenth | A seventieth | A half | A hundredth
exercise: What is one idea for why years seem to fly by for older people? | Each year is a smaller part of their whole life | The Earth spins faster and faster for older people | Older people's clocks run faster | Years really get shorter over time
exercise: Mila's car trip is 3 hours. If she asks "are we there yet?" every 10 minutes, how many times does she ask? | 18 | 30 | 3 | 13
exercise: Tom's scary 30-minute swimming lesson feels like the longest half hour of his life. What is true? | It was 30 minutes; fear made it feel longer | It really lasted much longer than 30 minutes | Fear makes clocks run more slowly | It lasted less than 30 minutes
exercise: How many days are there from one Christmas to the next? | 365, or 366 in a leap year | 360 days in every single year | Fewer days each year as you grow | 52 days in every year, one per week
exercise: Kai's Fortnite match and his homework both take 20 minutes. Which one is really longer? | Neither; both are 20 minutes | The homework, because it is boring | The Fortnite match, because it's fun | It depends on whose clock you look at
exercise: How do scientists study the feeling of time? | They ask people to guess how long things took | They stop all the clocks in a room | They measure how fast people's hearts beat at night | They count how many clocks people own
test: Maya swears, "I only played for 10 minutes!" Clock: 4:00 to 5:10. How much is she out by? | 60 minutes | 10 minutes | 70 minutes | 50 minutes
test: Amir: "I've had 6 days in Minecraft and it's only been 2 hours!" Is his maths right if a Minecraft day is 20 minutes? | Yes, 120 ÷ 20 = 6 | No, it should be 12 days | No, it should be 2 days | No, it should be 20 days
test: What would happen to Amir's count if a Minecraft day lasted 10 minutes instead of 20, over the same 2 hours? | He would have 12 days instead of 6 | He would have 3 days instead of 6 | He would still have 6 days | He would have 20 days instead of 6
test: Ivy: "Christmas can't come quicker, Mum, it's always 365 days." Mum: "It feels quicker every year." Who's right? | Both: Ivy about the length, Mum about the feeling | Only Ivy; feelings about time are made up | Only Mum; the year really is shorter | Neither; the year is 52 days long
test: Grandad, an old-school boomer (an older person): "Back in my day a summer lasted forever, now a year's gone in a blink." What's a good scientific answer? | One idea is that each year is a smaller part of his life, and fewer things are new | Summers were really longer when he was young | The Earth now goes round the Sun faster | Clocks today tick faster than old ones
test: A dreich wet Sunday with nothing to do feels endless. A day at the beach flies past. Both days have how many hours? | 24 hours each | More hours on the dull day | More hours on the beach day | It changes with the weather
test: Kai: "Homework's 20 minutes? That's forever. Fortnite's 20 minutes? That's quick." What's a clever way to beat this? | Time the homework once and see it is only 20 minutes | Play Fortnite during the homework | Do homework only when the clock is fast | Never start homework, because it never ends
test: Lexi says, "The dentist made the clock slow, it's giving broken clock." What's really true? | The clock was fine; worry made the wait feel long | The dentist really slowed the clock down | The waiting room clock runs on a different time zone | All clocks run slow near dentists
test: Mila asks "are we there yet?" every 10 minutes on a 3-hour drive. Dad: "Ask one more time and you're walking!" If she asks every 20 minutes instead, how many times will she ask? | 9 | 18 | 6 | 20
test: Oscar's brother: "50 years an hour, that's a year a minute, sound." Is "a year a minute" exactly right? | Not quite; it's a bit less than a year a minute | Yes, exactly one year a minute | No, it's 50 years a minute | No, it's about one year every hour, not every minute
test: Tom, after his scary lesson: "That half hour lasted two hours, I swear." Why might his memory say so? | In a fright the brain stores lots of details, so it seems longer | Scary lessons really last two hours | His watch stopped while he was scared | Water makes time go slower for swimmers
test: A cartoon says a magic clock runs faster when you're happy. What is true in real life? | Clocks keep the same speed; only our feeling of time changes | Happy people make clocks run faster | Sad people make clocks run backwards | Clocks speed up whenever music plays
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the speed of a clock during an enjoyable activity. | The clock proceeds at its customary rate; only the perception alters | The clock accelerates in proportion to the enjoyment that is experienced | The clock ceases to operate until the activity ends | The clock proceeds at half its customary rate
exam: It is given that an in-game day endures twenty real minutes. Determine the number of such days contained within two real hours. | Six | Two | Twelve | Forty
exam: Whereas a journey occupies three hours and a question is posed every ten minutes, ascertain the total number of questions. | Eighteen | Thirty | Thirteen | Three
exam: Identify one proposed explanation for the perception that years pass more swiftly with advancing age. | Each year constitutes a smaller fraction of the life already lived | The Earth's orbit grows shorter with each decade | The clocks of older persons operate more rapidly | The calendar removes days for persons over seventy
